<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Versailles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Versailles is a town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the site of the vast palace built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, which became the seat of the French court and a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. In 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1919 the peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was signed in the same hall, marking the end of the German Empire's era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/versailles.jpg" alt="Versailles" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ToucanWings · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_a%C3%A9rienne_du_domaine_de_Versailles_par_ToucanWings_-_Creative_Commons_By_Sa_3.0_-_081_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under King Narai (r. 1656–1688) the court engaged actively with Europe: the Greek adventurer Constantine Phaulkon rose to high office, a French embassy under the Chevalier de Chaumont arrived in 1685, and the Siamese envoy Kosa Pan was received by Louis XIV at <b>Versailles</b> in 1686.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first empire relied on chartered companies, appointed governors, and trading monopolies loosely supervised from <b>Versailles</b>, with little institutional coherence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The German Empire was proclaimed in the Hall of Mirrors at <b>Versailles</b> on 18 January 1871, a deliberate humiliation of France during Prussia's decisive victory in the Franco-Prussian War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XIV then governed personally from 1661 with a small council and no chief minister, gathered the high nobility at <b>Versailles</b>, where the court settled in 1682, and never summoned the Estates-General, which did not meet between 1614 and 1789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Above all, Prussia unified Germany on its own terms, excluding Austria, through the wars of 1864–1871 and the proclamation of William I as German Emperor at <b>Versailles</b> on 18 January 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Policy was set at <b>Versailles</b> by the Ministry of the Marine, and letters took months to cross the Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
